<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    The scanner is a nested application rather than a spawned executable so
    that macOS can name it. A camera prompt has to say who is asking, and a
    bare helper spawned by Keep Vault is answered for by Keep Vault — which
    macOS cannot resolve to this bundle, because the bundle's main executable
    is the launcher while the process that actually runs is the core. With no
    name available the request was refused outright instead of prompting.

    As its own bundle the helper answers for itself: its own identifier, its
    own usage description, and its own switch under Privacy. The camera stays
    out of the core, which is the point of having a helper at all.
  -->
  <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
  <string>de.michael-feinermann.keep-vault.scanner</string>
  <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
  <string>Keep Vault Scanner</string>
  <key>CFBundleName</key>
  <string>Keep Vault Scanner</string>
  <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
  <string>Keep Vault Scanner</string>
  <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
  <string>APPL</string>
  <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
  <string>6.0</string>
  <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
  <string>@@BUILD_VERSION@@</string>
  <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
  <string>@@BUILD_VERSION@@</string>
  <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
  <string>14.0</string>
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Keep Vault liest den QR-Code eines gedruckten Schlüsselzettels, damit der 512-Bit-Faktor nicht von Hand eingetippt werden muss. Das Kamerabild wird nur währenddessen ausgewertet und nirgends gespeichert.</string>
  <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
  <true/>
  <!--
    No Dock icon and no menu bar: the helper is a single window that exists for
    the seconds a scan takes, not an application the user switches to.
  -->
  <key>LSUIElement</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
